                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "If you're looking for a decent Metroidvania without any particularly brutal difficulty spikes, and one that's primarily designed around local co-op, give this game a shot.\n\nClearly and unmistakably inspired by the '90s anime Æon Flux, this game doesn't reinvent the genre, nor does it aspire to become one of its defining entries. What it offers instead is a solid experience, especially when played with someone else. You can play solo, but honestly, you won't have even a third as much fun.\n\nLet's start with the good stuff: graphics, art direction, soundtrack, and fluid combat. You control two characters, one wielding a sword and the other a bow, and battles can be a blast when you coordinate your attacks properly.\n\nAs for the areas that could use some improvement, the map and UI would definitely benefit from a few quality-of-life features, such as the ability to leave markers and reminders. I won't even tell you what it was like having to retrace my steps across the entire map to collect all the collectibles.\n\nExploration and progression are a little too linear and formulaic, even if they're competently executed. The difficulty is also pretty tame, aside from a few rough patches early on. Most bosses went down on our first attempt. The final boss, the only one with two phases, took us two.\n\nOverall, Anima Flux is an enjoyable, well-paced adventure of respectable quality, provided you can overlook a few flaws. Its biggest issue is an underlying imbalance in its difficulty design: the bosses offer very little challenge, while the lack of quality-of-life features and a number of frustrating obstacles seem designed to artificially raise the difficulty rather than create a naturally satisfying challenge.\n\nThe storytelling, which relies mostly on collectible notes scattered throughout the map, is also rather underwhelming.\n\nIf you're in the mood for a fun co-op adventure that's neither too long nor too demanding, and you aren't expecting another Hollow Knight, I might even recommend it. Just don't pay full price.",
